<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">2 July in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 2 July, from 626 to 1964.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/626">626 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,400 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Li Shimin kills his brothers in the Xuanwu Gate Incident</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a> ambushed and killed his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jiancheng">Li Jiancheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanji">Li Yuanji</a> at the Xuanwu Gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. Within two months his father abdicated, and as Emperor Taizong he presided over a golden age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanwu-gate-incident">Xuanwu Gate Incident</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jiancheng">Li Jiancheng</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1776">1776 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">250 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Continental Congress votes for independence from Britain</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Congress adopted the Lee Resolution declaring the colonies free and independent states, with twelve in favour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> abstaining. John Adams predicted that 2 July would be celebrated for ever; posterity chose the 4th.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1863">1863 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">163 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Union troops hold Little Round Top on Gettysburg's second day</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Confederate assaults struck the Union left at Little Round Top, the Wheatfield and the Peach Orchard. The Union line held its high ground by a narrow margin, setting the stage for Pickett's Charge the next day.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1900">1900 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">126 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">First Zeppelin airship flies over Lake Constance</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-zeppelin">Ferdinand von Zeppelin</a>'s LZ 1 made its maiden flight from a floating hangar on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. It was the first flight of a rigid airship, beginning an era that ended with the Hindenburg disaster of 1937.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1937">1937 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">89 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Amelia Earhart disappears over the Pacific</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Amelia Earhart and navigator Fred Noonan vanished while searching for tiny Howland Island on the last long legs of their round-the-world flight. Despite a vast search, their fate has never been conclusively established.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1964">1964 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">62 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Lyndon Johnson signs the Civil Rights Act</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">President Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act, outlawing discrimination on the basis of race, colour, religion, sex or national origin. It ended legal segregation in public places and was the most sweeping civil rights law since Reconstruction.</p></div></li></ol>
